\title{The Topology of Knotted Light: Alexander Polynomials in the Orbital-Angular-Momentum Spectrum}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Structured laser beams can carry a \emph{phase singularity}---a curve along which
the complex field amplitude vanishes---and that curve can be knotted. Such
\emph{knotted light} beams also carry orbital angular momentum (OAM), and a
recurring conjecture in singular optics holds that the beam's OAM spectrum is
governed by the \emph{Alexander polynomial} $\Delta_K$ of the underlying knot $K$:
the quantized OAM values $\ell$ are exactly those for which $\Delta_K$ vanishes on
the root of unity $e^{2\pi i \ell/N}$, where $N$ is a modular period associated
with the knot. We isolate and prove the exact algebra behind the conjecture for
the four smallest knots. The trefoil ($3_1$) and cinquefoil ($5_1$) Alexander
polynomials are the sixth and tenth cyclotomic polynomials, so their roots are
roots of unity and produce clean OAM quantization at $\ell \equiv 1,5 \pmod 6$ and
$\ell \equiv 1,3,7,9 \pmod{10}$; the unknot has empty spectrum; and the
figure-eight ($4_1$) has Alexander roots $\varphi^{\pm 2} = (3\pm\sqrt5)/2$---the
squares of the golden ratio---lying \emph{off} the unit circle, so it admits no
root-of-unity OAM quantization. We also establish the reciprocity (palindromic)
functional equation, the normalization $\Delta(1)=\pm1$, and the knot determinants
$|\Delta(-1)| = 3,5,5$.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

A monochromatic optical field is described by a complex scalar amplitude
$\psi(\mathbf{x})$. Generically the zero set $\{\psi = 0\}$ is a one-dimensional
curve in three-dimensional space---a \emph{phase singularity}, or optical vortex
line, along which the field's phase is undefined. It is possible to engineer beams
in which this vortex line is \emph{knotted}. Independently, a field with azimuthal
phase dependence $e^{i\ell\theta}$ carries orbital angular momentum (OAM)
$\ell\hbar$ per photon, with $\ell \in \Z$. A knotted beam is a superposition of
OAM eigenmodes and thus possesses an \emph{OAM spectrum}.

The organizing conjecture is that the spectrum is dictated by the topology of the
vortex knot through its Alexander polynomial:
\begin{equation}
\text{OAM spectrum of a beam with vortex knot } K = \{\, \ell : \Delta_K(e^{2\pi i \ell/N}) = 0 \,\}. \tag{$\star$}
\end{equation}
The content of $(\star)$ is determined by the location of the roots of $\Delta_K$
relative to the unit circle. We prove the relevant root structure for the smallest
knots and thereby determine, in each case, whether $(\star)$ yields genuine
quantized OAM.

\section{Background: knots and the Alexander polynomial}

A \emph{knot} is an embedding of $S^1$ into $S^3$, up to ambient isotopy. The
\emph{Alexander polynomial} $\Delta_K(t) \in \Z[t,t^{-1}]$ is a classical isotopy
invariant, definable from a Seifert matrix $V$ via $\Delta_K(t) \doteq \det(V -
tV^{\mathsf T})$. Normalized to a polynomial with nonzero constant term:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Knot & Symbol & $\Delta_K(t)$ \\
\midrule
Unknot & $0_1$ & $1$ \\
Trefoil & $3_1$ & $t^2 - t + 1$ \\
Figure-eight & $4_1$ & $t^2 - 3t + 1$ \\
Cinquefoil & $5_1$ & $t^4 - t^3 + t^2 - t + 1$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Every knot satisfies reciprocity $t^{\deg\Delta}\Delta(1/t) = \Delta(t)$ and
normalization $\Delta(1) = \pm 1$; the \emph{knot determinant} is $\det(K) =
|\Delta_K(-1)|$, an odd positive integer.

\section{The OAM spectrum}

We model the Alexander polynomial as an entire function $\Delta : \C \to \C$ and
place OAM labels on the real line.

\begin{definition}[OAM spectrum]
For an Alexander polynomial $\Delta$ and a modular period $N \in \N$,
\[
\OAM(\Delta, N) = \Big\{\, \ell \in \R : \Delta\big(e^{2\pi i \ell/N}\big) = 0 \,\Big\}.
\]
\end{definition}

Since $e^{2\pi i \ell/N}$ always lies on the unit circle, only roots of $\Delta$
\emph{on the unit circle} can contribute to the spectrum. We use the evaluation
forms $\Delta_{\text{unknot}}(z) = 1$, $\Delta_{3_1}(z) = z^2 - z + 1$, and
$\Delta_{5_1}(z) = z^4 - z^3 + z^2 - z + 1$.

\section{The trefoil: sixth roots of unity}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:tref}
If $z \in \C$ satisfies $z^3 = -1$ and $z \neq -1$, then $\Delta_{3_1}(z) = z^2 -
z + 1 = 0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The identity $(z+1)(z^2 - z + 1) = z^3 + 1$ holds in any commutative ring.
Substituting $z^3 = -1$ gives $(z+1)(z^2 - z + 1) = 0$. As $z \neq -1$, the
cofactor vanishes.
\end{proof}

Thus $\Delta_{3_1}$ is the cofactor of $(t+1)$ in $t^3 + 1$, i.e.\ the sixth
cyclotomic polynomial $\Phi_6(t) = t^2 - t + 1$. Let $\zeta_6 = e^{2\pi i/6}$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:z6}
$\zeta_6 \neq -1$ and $\zeta_6^3 = -1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By primitivity $\zeta_6^2 \neq 1$, so $\zeta_6 \neq -1$. From $\zeta_6^6 = 1$,
$(\zeta_6^3)^2 = 1$, so $\zeta_6^3 = \pm 1$; and $\zeta_6^3 \neq 1$, so $\zeta_6^3
= -1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Trefoil spectrum]
$1, 5 \in \OAM(\Delta_{3_1}, 6)$ and $0 \notin \OAM(\Delta_{3_1}, 6)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $\ell = 1$: $e^{2\pi i/6} = \zeta_6$; Lemmas~\ref{lem:tref}--\ref{lem:z6} give
$\Delta_{3_1}(\zeta_6) = 0$. For $\ell = 5$: $e^{2\pi i \cdot 5/6} = \zeta_6^5$,
and $(\zeta_6^5)^3 = (\zeta_6^6)^2\zeta_6^3 = -1$ with $\zeta_6^5 \neq -1$ (its
square $\zeta_6^4 \neq 1$), so Lemma~\ref{lem:tref} applies. For $\ell = 0$:
$\Delta_{3_1}(1) = 1 \neq 0$.
\end{proof}

The roots $e^{\pm i\pi/3}$ are the primitive sixth roots of unity, confirming
$\Delta_{3_1} = \Phi_6$. The trefoil is the $(2,3)$ torus knot.

\begin{theorem}[Trivial spectrum]
For every $N$, $\OAM(\Delta_{\text{unknot}}, N) = \varnothing$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\Delta_{\text{unknot}} \equiv 1$ never vanishes.
\end{proof}

\section{The cinquefoil: tenth roots of unity}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cinq}
If $z^5 = -1$ and $z \neq -1$, then $\Delta_{5_1}(z) = z^4 - z^3 + z^2 - z + 1 = 0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$(z+1)(z^4 - z^3 + z^2 - z + 1) = z^5 + 1 = 0$ and $z \neq -1$.
\end{proof}

So $\Delta_{5_1} = \Phi_{10}(t) = t^4 - t^3 + t^2 - t + 1$. Let $\zeta_{10} =
e^{2\pi i/10}$; as in Lemma~\ref{lem:z6}, $\zeta_{10} \neq -1$ and $\zeta_{10}^5 =
-1$.

\begin{theorem}[Cinquefoil spectrum]
$1 \in \OAM(\Delta_{5_1}, 10)$, and likewise $3,7,9 \in \OAM(\Delta_{5_1}, 10)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$e^{2\pi i/10} = \zeta_{10}$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:cinq} give
$\Delta_{5_1}(\zeta_{10}) = 0$. The values $\ell = 3,7,9$ correspond to the other
primitive tenth roots $\zeta_{10}^3, \zeta_{10}^7, \zeta_{10}^9$, each a fifth
root of $-1$ distinct from $-1$.
\end{proof}

The cinquefoil is the $(2,5)$ torus knot.

\section{The figure-eight: golden-ratio roots off the unit circle}

Here $\Delta_{4_1}(t) = t^2 - 3t + 1$; write $f(x) = x^2 - 3x + 1$. Let $\varphi =
(1+\sqrt5)/2$, $\psi = (1-\sqrt5)/2$, with $\varphi^2 = \varphi + 1$, $\psi^2 =
\psi + 1$, $\varphi\psi = -1$.

\begin{theorem}[Golden roots]
$f(\varphi^2) = f(\psi^2) = 0$; the roots are $\varphi^2 = (3+\sqrt5)/2$ and
$\psi^2 = (3-\sqrt5)/2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Using $\varphi^2 = \varphi + 1$,
\[
f(\varphi^2) = (\varphi^2)^2 - 3\varphi^2 + 1 = (\varphi^2 + \varphi - 1)(\varphi^2 - \varphi - 1) = 0,
\]
since $\varphi^2 - \varphi - 1 = 0$. Identically for $\psi$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reciprocal, off-circle roots]
$\varphi^2\psi^2 = 1$ and $\varphi^2 > 1 > \psi^2 > 0$; neither root lies on the
unit circle.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\varphi^2\psi^2 = (\varphi\psi)^2 = 1$. Since $\varphi > 1$, $\varphi^2 > 1$, and
$\psi^2 = 1/\varphi^2 \in (0,1)$. Both are real and $\neq \pm 1$.
\end{proof}

Because $\OAM(\Delta, N)$ detects only unit-circle roots, the figure-eight admits
no root-of-unity OAM quantization: it is the smallest knot for which the naive
form of $(\star)$ gives an empty quantized spectrum despite being nontrivial. The
signature $(3\pm\sqrt5)/2$ is the tell-tale fingerprint.

\section{Structural invariants}

\begin{theorem}[Reciprocity]
For $z \neq 0$, $z^2\Delta_{3_1}(1/z) = \Delta_{3_1}(z)$ and $z^4\Delta_{5_1}(1/z)
= \Delta_{5_1}(z)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$z^2(z^{-2} - z^{-1} + 1) = z^2 - z + 1$; and $z^4(z^{-4} - z^{-3} + z^{-2} -
z^{-1} + 1) = z^4 - z^3 + z^2 - z + 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Normalization]
$\Delta_{3_1}(1) = 1$, $\Delta_{4_1}(1) = -1$, $\Delta_{5_1}(1) = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Direct evaluation. In each case $\Delta(1) = \pm 1$, so $e^0 = 1$ is never a root
and $\ell = 0$ is never quantized.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Knot determinants]
$\Delta_{3_1}(-1) = 3$, $\Delta_{4_1}(-1) = 5$, $\Delta_{5_1}(-1) = 5$; all odd.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$(-1)^2 - (-1) + 1 = 3$; $1 + 3 + 1 = 5$; $1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 5$.
\end{proof}

Hence $\det(3_1) = 3$, $\det(4_1) = 5$, $\det(5_1) = 5$. The trefoil's $\det = 3$
recovers its classical tricolorability.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The topology of a beam's vortex knot is legible in a measurable OAM spectrum, and
because topological data is perturbation-robust, knot-labeled OAM values inherit
that robustness. Our theorems draw a sharp line: cyclotomic knots ($3_1, 5_1$)
have all Alexander roots on the unit circle and yield crisp quantized OAM, while
the figure-eight's golden-ratio roots lie off the circle and yield none. Future
work: (1) prove $\Delta_{3_1} = \Phi_6$ and $\Delta_{5_1} = \Phi_{10}$ including
the converse; (2) the general torus-knot family $\Delta_{(2,2k+1)}(t) = (t^{2k+1}
+ 1)/(t+1)$; (3) abstract reciprocity and $\Delta(1) = \pm 1$ from Seifert
matrices; (4) knot determinant as $|\Delta(-1)|$ and $p$-colorability; (5)
characterize knots with all Alexander roots on the unit circle, the figure-eight
being the smallest violator.

